    "created_at": "2025-03-27T20:56:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-03-27T20:56:04Z",
    "body": "We've wrestled with this question in the past, and chromium's behavior has not always been consistent. I do agree that intuitively it makes sense that a display:none iframe wouldn't run rAF callbacks.\n\nOn a practical level, though, not running rAF callbacks in this situation conflicts with a particular web design pattern called \"brain frame\" that I've seen repeatedly on complex pages (e.g. gmail). The idea is that there are multiple frames/windows, possibly from multiple origins/domains, that work cooperatively to form a single app. There is typically one controlling iframe (the \"brain frame\") which coordinates the state between all the constituent windows of the app, and the brain frame is typically hidden from view. When we tried disabling rAF callbacks on display:none iframes in the past, we got immediate feedback from multiple sites that were broken.",
